# Rug plots: geom_rug

```elixir
Mix.install([
  {:giggly, path: Path.join(__DIR__, "..")},
  {:kino, "~> 0.19"}
  ])

alias Giggly.Plot

mpg = Giggly.Datasets.mpg()
mtcars = Giggly.Datasets.mtcars()

render = fn plot ->
  plot
  |> Giggly.to_svg()
  |> IO.iodata_to_binary()
  |> Kino.Image.new(:svg)
end
```

## About this notebook

This notebook follows the examples on ggplot2's reference page for
[`geom_rug()`](https://ggplot2.tidyverse.org/reference/geom_rug.html), in the order the page
gives them. Each example shows ggplot2's code first and then Giggly's. An example Giggly cannot
draw yet keeps its ggplot2 code and names what it is waiting for, so the page is also a list of
what remains.

A rug plot adds the two one-dimensional distributions to a two-dimensional display, such as a
scatter plot: a short line at the edge of the panel for each row's `x`, and another for its `y`. It
draws every row, so it suits small tables. Giggly draws rugs with `Giggly.Plot.geom_rug/3`, along
the sides `sides:` names, as long as `length:` says, inside the panel or, with `outside: true`,
outside it, and jittered as `Giggly.Plot.geom_jitter/3` jitters points. A plot is described with
`Giggly.Plot` and drawn with `Giggly.to_svg/2`, which returns an SVG document. The setup cell
aliases `Giggly.Plot`, so the cells write `Plot.new/2`, and defines `render`, which draws a plot and
shows it below the cell with `Kino.Image`. The cells draw `Giggly.Datasets.mtcars/0`, R's `mtcars`:
32 cars from the 1974 *Motor Trend* US magazine, and `Giggly.Datasets.mpg/0`, ggplot2's `mpg`: fuel
economy for 234 cars of 38 popular models from 1999 to 2008, which the setup cell binds to `mtcars`
and `mpg`, R's names for them.

## A scatter plot

```r
p <- ggplot(mtcars, aes(wt, mpg)) +
  geom_point()
p
```

```elixir
p =
  mtcars
  |> Plot.new(%{x: :wt, y: :mpg})
  |> Plot.geom_point()

render.(p)
```

## Rugs along the sides

```r
p + geom_rug()
p + geom_rug(sides="b")    # Rug on bottom only
p + geom_rug(sides="trbl") # All four sides
```

By default the rug runs along the bottom and the left, `sides = "bl"`. Giggly's `sides:` is a
list of the sides, in any order, where ggplot2's is a string of their first letters.

```elixir
p
|> Plot.geom_rug()
|> render.()
```

```elixir
p
|> Plot.geom_rug(sides: [:bottom])
|> render.()
```

```elixir
p
|> Plot.geom_rug(sides: [:top, :right, :bottom, :left])
|> render.()
```

## Jittering the rug

```r
# Use jittering to avoid overplotting for smaller datasets
ggplot(mpg, aes(displ, cty)) +
  geom_point() +
  geom_rug()

ggplot(mpg, aes(displ, cty)) +
  geom_jitter() +
  geom_rug(alpha = 1/2, position = "jitter")
```

`displ` and `cty` take few distinct values, so many cars share a point and a rug line. Where a
rug runs along both axes, cars sharing both values are drawn once on each side, as ggplot2 draws
them, so the first plot's 234 cars draw 100 lines along each side.

```elixir
mpg
|> Plot.new(%{x: :displ, y: :cty})
|> Plot.geom_point()
|> Plot.geom_rug()
|> render.()
```

Jittering moves each car by a small random amount, so they can be told apart. The points are
jittered by Erlang's random numbers, not R's, so they land elsewhere than ggplot2's, each within the
same 0.04 litres of its place across and 0.4 miles per gallon up or down, and the axes, which widen
to hold them, can end a little elsewhere too, by no more than about 0.08 litres and 0.8 miles per
gallon; nothing else in the drawing moves (`geom_jitter()`'s notebook says more). The rug is
jittered from the same seed as the points, so each of its lines marks a point, where ggplot2 draws
new random numbers for each layer and its rug's lines fall beside the points.

```elixir
mpg
|> Plot.new(%{x: :displ, y: :cty})
|> Plot.geom_jitter()
|> Plot.geom_rug(alpha: 0.5, position: :jitter)
|> render.()
```

## Rugs outside the panel

```r
# move the rug tassels to outside the plot
# remember to set clip = "off".
p +
  geom_rug(outside = TRUE) +
  coord_cartesian(clip = "off")
```

The panel cuts off what is drawn past its edges, so lines running outside it show only where
`Giggly.Plot.coord_cartesian/2` is given `clip: :off`.

```elixir
p
|> Plot.geom_rug(outside: true)
|> Plot.coord_cartesian(clip: :off)
|> render.()
```

## Rugs on the top and right

```r
# set sides to top right, and then move the margins
p +
  geom_rug(outside = TRUE, sides = "tr") +
  coord_cartesian(clip = "off") +
  theme(plot.margin = margin_auto(1, unit = "cm"))
```

A centimetre of margin on every side leaves the lines room past the panel's top and right edges.
Giggly writes the margin `margin_auto(1, unit = "cm")` gives as one length, `{1, :cm}`.

```elixir
p
|> Plot.geom_rug(outside: true, sides: [:top, :right])
|> Plot.coord_cartesian(clip: :off)
|> Plot.theme(plot_margin: {1, :cm})
|> render.()
```

## Longer rug lines

```r
# increase the line length and
# expand axis to avoid overplotting
p +
  geom_rug(length = unit(0.05, "npc")) +
  scale_y_continuous(expand = c(0.1, 0.1))
```

A rug's lines are 3% of the panel long by default; `{0.05, :npc}` makes them 5%, as `unit(0.05,
"npc")` does. ggplot2 reads the two numbers of `expand = c(0.1, 0.1)` as a multiple of the axis's
range and a distance in its units, left at each end, which Giggly writes `[mult: 0.1, add: 0.1]`.

```elixir
p
|> Plot.geom_rug(length: {0.05, :npc})
|> Plot.scale_y_continuous(expand: [mult: 0.1, add: 0.1])
|> render.()
```
